.SS Abstract
Modern CPUs commonly use separate \f[B]data\f[R] and
\f[B]instruction\f[R] caches.
.PP
When you write executable memory (e.g., JIT, trampolines, runtime
patching), the write updates the \f[B]data cache\f[R] only.
The \f[B]instruction cache\f[R] may still hold stale instructions for
the same address range.
.PP
Without explicit synchronization, the CPU can execute outdated or
invalid instructions.
.PP
After invoking the macro:
.IP \(bu 2
Data cache lines covering \f[CR][begin, end)\f[R] are written back (if
required by architecture)
.IP \(bu 2
Instruction cache lines covering \f[CR][begin, end)\f[R] are invalidated
.IP \(bu 2
Subsequent execution fetches updated instructions from memory
.PP
\f[B]Architecture notes:\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]x86 / x86_64\f[R]: Typically coherent; often no visible effect
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ARM / RISC\-V / MIPS / PowerPC\f[R]: Explicit cache maintenance
required
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS Content
.IP
.EX
#define CLEAR_INSTRUCTION_CACHE(BEGIN, END)
#define clear_instruction_cache(begin, end)
.EE
.PP
Forces instruction cache synchronization for the address range
\f[CR][BEGIN, END)\f[R].
.PP
Both macro variants are provided for stylistic preference.
They perform identical operations.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]BEGIN\f[R] \- start address (inclusive)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]END\f[R] \- end address (exclusive)
.PP
Intended for:
.IP \(bu 2
JIT compilers
.IP \(bu 2
Runtime code generation
.IP \(bu 2
Hot patching
.IP \(bu 2
Trampoline injection
.IP \(bu 2
Self\-modifying code
.PP
\f[B]Examples:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
memcpy(code_ptr, bytes, size);
CLEAR_INSTRUCTION_CACHE(code_ptr, code_ptr + size);
mprotect(page, RX);
.EE
.PP
Call it \f[B]after writing executable bytes\f[R] and \f[B]before
execution\f[R].
.PP
Failure to do so on non\-coherent architectures results in undefined
behavior.
.PP
Another example:
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<stdio.h>\f[R]
#include \f[B]<CMT/OS_API/CLEAR_INSTRUCTION_CACHE.H>\f[R]

void foo(void)
{
    printf(\(dqHello before cache flush\(rsn\(dq);
}

int main(void) {
    void *begin = (void *)&foo;
    void *end = (void *)((char *)&foo + 64); \f[I]// fake range\f[R]

    CLEAR_INSTRUCTION_CACHE(begin, end);

    foo();

    \f[B]return\f[R] (0);
}
.EE
